Z||000003||Mattyman||12-29-2001||05:19 PM||||Ok, I just took the car out to see if everything is working alright.  The heat now works fine, but the temp gauge gets kinda high. It was always on the mark between 100 and 220 when I would just start up the car and it would stay around there.  I just went around the block and it went to the 220 mark and the fan did not kick on.  What is the problem here, i'm thinking of taking the thermostat back out and living with no heat.  What can I do?||||reg||1
Z||000004||JohnF||12-29-2001||05:48 PM||||Give the forum something ot work with!  <P>Year model, engine, a/c?<P>SERVICE ENGINE light? Codes?<P>With engine running, does fan come on when a/c is turned on?||||reg||4
Z||000005||Formula88||12-29-2001||05:54 PM||||Well, on my Formula, the fan doesn't come on until 235*F and it shuts off about 200-210*F (don't rembmer the off temp exactly).  This is normal and how it should work.  I've since added a Rodney Dickman low temp fan switch and 180* thermostat, and my car never runs over 200*F now.  I love it!  Oh, and I still get plenty of heat.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Mattyman||12-29-2001||07:14 PM||||The car is an 87 4cyl. There is no check engine light or anything like that. I was just worried because the temperature goes up higher to around 220. Thats more than it usually did.  The fan does come on with the air conditioning on.||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||12-30-2001||02:51 PM||||87-88 4 cyl<P>The ECM controls the fan and it is programmed for 225 on and 210 off.<P>220 is a bit warm... If it's holding there then you could have some air in the system or a flow rate problem. It's also posible there's nothing wrong... how fast were you driving? Slow driving will run it warmer... <P>The only sure way to check the temp is with an ECM scanner, which reads the more accurate sensor used by the ECM, or with a pyrometer at the Tstat neck. That need to be done at least once to make certain the dash gauge is accurate. The dash gauge is often screwed up due to being pounded by the wiring problem that makes it peg durring starting.<P>If you suspect air or flow problems....<P>Check for a pinched pipe/hose. <P>If the coolant was neglected then the radiator could be crapped up.<P>check all hose joints. including under the car. there are some crimped ones that may be splitting.<P>I'd recomend you pull the cover off the heater core and physically inspect for signs of it leaking. It can weap long before it starts dripping obvious amounts on the floor etc. (Mine did that... )<P>With a "perfect" cooling system, my 87SC (4cyl) hardly every runs much over 200 even in slow traffic. I run a 195 Tstat... It has to be really hot out with slow traffic or idled while parked to get to 220. <P>I'm also running Mobil 1 which helps... (better oil, less friction, cooler motor.) I think with regular oil I ran about 200-210 most of the time with a 195stat. In slow traffic, it would push 220 fairly easy. (All these numbers are from my ECM scanner.)<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000008||blakeinspace||12-31-2001||01:31 PM||||"The dash gauge is often screwed up due to being pounded by the wiring problem that makes it peg during starting."<P>Hey ogre...  I suspect that is the case on my FORM, after warm-up my guage only registers at the 1st tick mark.  Somethimes the darn thing pegs itself up in the noon or 1 o'clock positions (and stays).  ANY way to fix this?  And BTW, your suggestions for door sill clean up worked. variable speed dremel and 3 step Mcguire's polish worked nicely.  Thanks<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||rogergarrison||12-31-2001||01:49 PM||||make sure you filled coolant the proper way to keep from having an airlock. 220 is not bad for stock thermostat. My corvette runs around 225 all the time, fan doesnt even go on till almost 230. I wondered and ask about that, other owners and dealer told me thats normal. Lots of them put in the 160/180 thermotstat and reprogram fans just like us.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Mattyman||12-31-2001||03:53 PM||||Well, my temp. gauge is messed up sometimes it gets pegged the whole way on the left side then when I restart the car it will work again.  Everytime I start the car the needle always goes to the first hash mark between the 100 and 220 and works it way over from there.  Not once did it ever read between the 100 and first hash mark.  I was also driving pretty slow when I was testing the car.  I was going at most 40 and averaged somewhere around 30mph.  Also how can I flush the coolant system.  My dad probably knows how but i'm not that good with fixing cars yet.  I also noticed that when my car is cold during the winter time that when I first start it up it idles high at around 2grand then after it warms up it falls back down. It never did anything like that in the summer when it was warm.  I just want to make sure the car is fine because it's in real good shape and really had no problems before.<P>------------------<BR>You gotta stand for Something or your gonna fall for everything.<p>[This message has been edited by Mattyman (edited 12-31-2001).]||||reg||1
